WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.293 --> 00:00:04.170
I'm constantly checking the time.

00:00:06.506 --> 00:00:08.800
Sometimes for no real reason at all.

00:00:11.386 --> 00:00:14.848
And I think it's because I usually feel
like I don't have enough time.

00:00:15.765 --> 00:00:19.519
And I guess this is my attempt
to control it.

00:00:20.562 --> 00:00:22.689
But this little habit has turned me

00:00:22.689 --> 00:00:25.692
into someone that I don't really like.

00:00:28.236 --> 00:00:29.612
So when I started

00:00:29.612 --> 00:00:33.408
working with my first client,
who basically gave me

00:00:33.575 --> 00:00:38.413
unlimited design freedom, I knew
I wanted to explore this idea of time

00:00:39.289 --> 00:00:42.292
and is there any way to slow it down?

00:00:46.087 --> 00:00:47.005
This video is sponsored

00:00:47.005 --> 00:00:49.382
by SketchUp and Morpholio.

00:00:50.341 --> 00:00:52.177
This feeling of being rushed.

00:00:52.177 --> 00:00:55.180
It's a universal feeling,
especially in the digital age.

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:58.641
But if you think about it, it's
actually kind of ironic.

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:02.854
All of these tools are designed
to increase efficiency

00:01:02.854 --> 00:01:06.566
so that we can have more time
and improve the quality of our lives.

00:01:06.816 --> 00:01:11.613
But instead we're all just pushing
for more, more productivity,

00:01:11.613 --> 00:01:15.825
more experiences, more excitement,
more things to see, more things to do.

00:01:15.825 --> 00:01:17.619
F.O.M.O. (FEAR OF MISSING OUT)

00:01:17.619 --> 00:01:20.955
But leisure in itself
has become a multi million dollar industry

00:01:20.955 --> 00:01:24.709
that tries to keep us
constantly entertained, constantly wanting

00:01:24.709 --> 00:01:28.838
more, and again making us feel like
we don't have enough time

00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:38.723
There's a few moments in my life

00:01:38.723 --> 00:01:42.727
where I felt the sensation
of being unplugged from the world.

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:47.565
There's a quality about these moments
that makes me want to slow down my pace,

00:01:48.066 --> 00:01:51.111
willing myself to be lost and to be alone.

00:01:51.111 --> 00:01:54.239
So when I started
designing the Orinda house (San Francisco, CA)

00:01:54.239 --> 00:01:57.242
I tried to let these memories
guide the design.

00:01:57.408 --> 00:02:00.537
In some ways,
I think this is kind of the perfect site.

00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:05.375
You come here to disconnect from
the rest of the world at the same time.

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:09.546
The site does come with a few challenges.

00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:12.757
Feels a little bit steeper than I thought

00:02:12.757 --> 00:02:15.760
it was going to be.

00:02:19.139 --> 00:02:22.475
So as you can see,
the hill is extremely steep.

00:02:22.475 --> 00:02:26.813
It's got almost a 30 degree
slope and it's facing north.

00:02:27.063 --> 00:02:28.148
So the hours of

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:31.776
daylight is actually quite limited
because it's blocked by this hill.

00:02:32.193 --> 00:02:36.364
In fact, when we did a proper Sun study,
the only part of the site that got

00:02:36.364 --> 00:02:40.535
lots of daylight throughout the day
is at the top of the hill right here.

00:02:40.869 --> 00:02:43.746
There's also like a million protected
trees on the site.

00:02:43.746 --> 00:02:48.001
Basically, anything over a six inch
diameter is a protected tree.

00:02:48.418 --> 00:02:52.463
So I had to pick a spot that would require
the least amount of tree cutting.

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:54.883
We also have this 25 foot height limit.

00:02:54.883 --> 00:02:58.136
So the lower we go down the site,
the shallower

00:02:58.136 --> 00:02:59.470
we have to make the floor plate.

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:04.100
Otherwise, we have to corner to the hill,
which based on our Geotech report,

00:03:04.559 --> 00:03:08.021
it will be possible,
but it will be a challenge

00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:09.898
because the bedrock is very hard.

00:03:09.898 --> 00:03:12.901
So this flat portion right here

00:03:12.901 --> 00:03:16.029
seemed like the ideal location
for the building.

00:03:16.446 --> 00:03:17.989
You get tons of daylight.

00:03:17.989 --> 00:03:20.533
There's not a ton of trees around.

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:26.122
And with our concept of maximizing time,
this location seemed like a no brainer.

00:03:29.417 --> 00:03:30.084
But the problem

00:03:30.084 --> 00:03:33.087
was, how do we get all the way up here?

00:03:33.338 --> 00:03:35.757
How do we get materials up here?

00:03:35.757 --> 00:03:39.469
Building
a driveway will be very expensive,

00:03:39.761 --> 00:03:42.805
and we'd have to cut down a lot of trees.

00:03:43.223 --> 00:03:46.309
And the city of Orinda would not be happy.

00:03:46.476 --> 00:03:48.978
The solution actually came from
an acquaintance.

00:03:48.978 --> 00:03:54.067
Apparently, these little hillside
lifts are quite common in California

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:57.070
because there's so many steep
sites like this.

00:03:57.070 --> 00:04:00.073
So the driveway and the garage,

00:04:00.198 --> 00:04:04.869
they can stay right here
at the bottom of the hill.

00:04:05.328 --> 00:04:11.376
And so you park your car right here
and you take your little hillside elevator

00:04:13.253 --> 00:04:14.420
and that's how

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:17.423
you get to the house
as you slowly move up the lift.

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:18.841
You sit.

00:04:18.841 --> 00:04:21.427
You look at the trees and you can also

00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:25.139
see glimpses of the building
when you finally reach the top.

00:04:25.515 --> 00:04:30.353
All you have is a platform
and a massive concrete wall on the side.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:32.897
And when you touch the wall,

00:04:32.897 --> 00:04:37.235
you're totally surprised
to find that it's warm and soft.

00:04:38.987 --> 00:04:41.698
In the
summer of 2016, we traveled across Europe

00:04:41.698 --> 00:04:44.701
to look at architecture
at the last leg of our trip.

00:04:44.742 --> 00:04:47.704
We made our way to this little Swiss
village

00:04:47.704 --> 00:04:51.291
called Head in State
to visit the home and studio.

00:04:51.332 --> 00:04:57.046
Peter Zumthor, who happens to be one of
my favorite architects, nobody was there.

00:04:57.213 --> 00:05:01.175
So technically
I guess we weren't actually visiting.

00:05:01.718 --> 00:05:05.638
But regardless, the building was designed
by Peter Zumthor himself.

00:05:06.097 --> 00:05:08.725
So I took like a million pictures.

00:05:08.725 --> 00:05:13.146
I peeked through the windows and I touched
everything I could get my hands on.

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:14.439
Don't ask why.

00:05:14.439 --> 00:05:16.649
It was just before we were about to leave.

00:05:16.649 --> 00:05:18.735
As I was trying
to get my last few pictures,

00:05:18.735 --> 00:05:23.531
I found a nice old window recessed
into a perfectly formed concrete.

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:26.743
But something was different
when I touched it.

00:05:27.368 --> 00:05:28.328
I was shocked.

00:05:28.328 --> 00:05:32.081
The concrete was soft and warm.

00:05:32.415 --> 00:05:35.960
This hard
material had the texture of fabric.

00:05:36.044 --> 00:05:39.088
I think the concrete must have been formed
using fabric

00:05:39.088 --> 00:05:42.091
as a formwork,
and it conducted heat from the sunlight.

00:05:42.258 --> 00:05:45.845
The only proof that I have of this
is this photo,

00:05:46.804 --> 00:05:48.890
which doesn't even come close

00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:51.893
to capturing this feeling.

00:05:51.934 --> 00:05:57.982
But sometimes when I touch a soft linen,
maybe one that's been sitting out

00:05:57.982 --> 00:06:01.986
in the sun for a long time,
it reminds me of this moment.

00:06:03.613 --> 00:06:04.822
The problem is that

00:06:04.822 --> 00:06:07.825
vision is the loudest of our senses,

00:06:07.867 --> 00:06:11.913
and so by limiting our view of the house,
we start to pay

00:06:11.913 --> 00:06:14.916
a little bit more attention
to our other senses.

00:06:15.041 --> 00:06:19.253
You could hear the water in the distance,
and when you touch the wall,

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:23.508
you're surprised to feel
the strange texture under your fingertips.

00:06:24.217 --> 00:06:27.637
You notice
your movements, the variation in the wall,

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:30.973
and maybe you start to slow down.

00:06:32.308 --> 00:06:35.186
Once you turn the corner,
you enter into a garden.

00:06:35.186 --> 00:06:38.106
And it's not just a garden,
it's a labyrinth.

00:06:38.106 --> 00:06:39.899
And you have to take this meandering

00:06:39.899 --> 00:06:42.902
path in order
to reach the entrance of the house.

00:06:43.361 --> 00:06:46.447
And I'm sure over time
the family would build in shortcuts.

00:06:46.447 --> 00:06:49.450
Maybe here and here and here.

00:06:49.617 --> 00:06:52.745
But the main road
takes you on a long journey.

00:06:53.079 --> 00:06:56.082
This very intentional complexity
of the path.

00:06:56.624 --> 00:07:00.586
It creates a sense of mystery, curiosity.

00:07:00.753 --> 00:07:04.507
Each new turn reveals a new moment,
a hidden corner,

00:07:04.966 --> 00:07:09.846
a carefully framed vehicle,
or maybe a really soft moss

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:13.975
here, getting lost and then found.

00:07:14.434 --> 00:07:16.394
That's the whole point.

00:07:16.394 --> 00:07:18.896
That feeling of being blindly led

00:07:18.896 --> 00:07:21.899
by your feet

00:07:25.903 --> 00:07:26.529
elaborates, We're

00:07:26.529 --> 00:07:30.283
actually used for meditation by priests
during the medieval times.

00:07:30.408 --> 00:07:33.911
They're non-linear
and they can be disorienting,

00:07:34.203 --> 00:07:37.206
but knowing that they'll eventually get
to the end.

00:07:37.248 --> 00:07:41.502
These long walks were used
to reach a state of contemplation.

00:07:41.836 --> 00:07:46.215
When you reach the end of the garden,
you land on a large stepping stone,

00:07:46.424 --> 00:07:49.177
which marks the end of the labyrinth
and the beginning

00:07:49.177 --> 00:07:50.178
of the house. 

00:07:57.226 --> 00:08:01.189
according to Juhani Pallasmaa,
images of water emphasize

00:08:01.189 --> 00:08:04.692
architectural permanence
and concretize the passing of time.

00:08:05.443 --> 00:08:07.945
The reflective surface of the water hides

00:08:07.945 --> 00:08:11.240
depth and projects a second hidden world.

00:08:11.491 --> 00:08:17.205
The double world activates our imagination
for the duality of past and future.

00:08:18.456 --> 00:08:19.707
So in order to get this kind

00:08:19.707 --> 00:08:22.919
of effect, the water needs to be
getting direct sunlight.

00:08:23.461 --> 00:08:26.589
The light hits, the water bounces off,

00:08:26.881 --> 00:08:29.717
and the refraction from the water
as projected

00:08:29.717 --> 00:08:32.720
onto this concrete wall.

00:08:33.471 --> 00:08:36.224
So after iterating
through a couple of different options,

00:08:36.224 --> 00:08:40.228
we decided to place the pools
at the top of the site.

00:08:40.228 --> 00:08:43.314
On the south end one reflecting pool

00:08:43.314 --> 00:08:46.317
here and one accessible pool here.

00:08:46.859 --> 00:08:49.862
And so we maximize the sunlight

00:08:50.029 --> 00:08:54.492
and this refraction of the water
becomes a part of the entry experience.

00:08:55.034 --> 00:08:57.995
So over here,
you can take a moment to pause.

00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:01.916
We landscaped this area so that
the seeding becomes a part of the hill.

00:09:02.166 --> 00:09:06.754
And instead of paving this area
with concrete, we continue the stone paths

00:09:06.754 --> 00:09:10.800
so that this entire space
feels like one continuous garden.

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:15.805
So when you enter the house,
you enter into a hall with wood slats

00:09:15.805 --> 00:09:20.142
on one side and a personalized concrete
wall on the other.

00:09:20.226 --> 00:09:23.563
The patterns on the concrete
panels create rhythm as you enter.

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:27.650
The entryway is a transitional place,
and it's a place of movement.

00:09:27.984 --> 00:09:30.987
And it's a place where outside becomes
inside.

00:09:31.445 --> 00:09:34.240
Is your first few moments into the house

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:37.326
where you leave your keys, your coat,

00:09:38.035 --> 00:09:42.540
where you leave your outside self behind
and you enter your home.

00:09:42.915 --> 00:09:46.711
It's not a moment,
but it's an in-between moment.

00:09:46.877 --> 00:09:49.880
And these are the moments
that usually get forgotten about.

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:52.883
These moments
make up only seconds in our days,

00:09:52.883 --> 00:09:56.220
but added up they become hours and days.

00:09:56.345 --> 00:10:00.850
And so by elevating this experience
with the light and texture,

00:10:01.434 --> 00:10:05.479
we can make even these transitional
moments memorable.

00:10:07.898 --> 00:10:08.232
As you

00:10:08.232 --> 00:10:12.069
descend the stairs through this narrow
transitional space,

00:10:12.278 --> 00:10:15.281
you experience expansion

00:10:16.699 --> 00:10:17.867
for the first time.

00:10:17.867 --> 00:10:20.870
You really see
the beautiful view over the hill,

00:10:21.454 --> 00:10:24.123
the interconnected spaces of the house,

00:10:24.123 --> 00:10:26.751
the reflection of the water on one side.

00:10:26.751 --> 00:10:30.463
And for a brief moment you take a pause.

00:10:34.717 --> 00:10:39.096
This isn't my typical process
for designing, but there was an object

00:10:39.096 --> 00:10:42.850
that kept coming to my mind
as I was designing the building a sundial.

00:10:43.184 --> 00:10:46.062
I think there's something really poetic
about it,

00:10:46.062 --> 00:10:48.606
how the hands stay still while it

00:10:48.606 --> 00:10:52.026
quietly witness
the fleeting hours and seasons.

00:10:52.276 --> 00:10:56.614
But compared to a digital clock, it's
not dictated by the seconds of minutes.

00:10:56.614 --> 00:11:00.242
And instead I feel a more gradual

00:11:00.284 --> 00:11:03.245
passing of time.

00:11:04.872 --> 00:11:11.170
Ha ha ha.

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:14.715
I think about the sun's journey
across the sky

00:11:14.965 --> 00:11:18.094
and it reminds me of our connection
to the universe.

00:11:18.344 --> 00:11:23.349
It makes me think about my existence
in the context of this vast space and time

00:11:27.228 --> 00:11:27.978
as the sun

00:11:27.978 --> 00:11:31.649
moves across the sky
from morning to evening.

00:11:31.899 --> 00:11:34.694
I wanted the building to kind of dance

00:11:34.694 --> 00:11:37.655
with the changing angles of the sunlight.

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:41.742
We tried a bunch of iterations of the roof
because it changed how the light

00:11:41.742 --> 00:11:45.913
stretched into the living room
and into the office and into the kitchen.

00:11:46.247 --> 00:11:50.209
Maybe this movement can even guide
some of the activity in the house.

00:11:54.422 --> 00:11:56.173
But there's another moment in the house

00:11:56.173 --> 00:11:59.260
where you experience light
in a totally different way,

00:11:59.885 --> 00:12:03.764
and that will be underneath the pool
right here

00:12:03.806 --> 00:12:06.350
where we get the refraction
through the water,

00:12:06.350 --> 00:12:09.311
which is a totally different
quality of light.

00:12:09.311 --> 00:12:12.648
This room actually drove
a lot of the relationships

00:12:12.648 --> 00:12:15.359
between the spaces in the house.

00:12:15.359 --> 00:12:19.363
I wanted the refraction to be a part
of the experience of the house

00:12:19.363 --> 00:12:23.075
so that the living room and the kitchen
can borrow some of that refraction.

00:12:23.617 --> 00:12:27.705
But because of the depth of the pool,
it was really hard to do that

00:12:27.705 --> 00:12:32.209
without placing the relaxation room
on a completely different basement level,

00:12:32.376 --> 00:12:36.630
which is why each of the spaces
stuck down a half storey.

00:12:36.797 --> 00:12:40.634
This creates separate spaces
that still feel interconnected

00:12:40.634 --> 00:12:43.721
and this works with the natural
topography of the site,

00:12:43.929 --> 00:12:47.391
which gradually steps down
as we move towards the east.

00:12:51.145 --> 00:12:55.149
Ha ha.

00:12:57.485 --> 00:13:00.404
Time is a human made invention.

00:13:00.404 --> 00:13:03.824
We create a time as a framework
to get organized

00:13:03.824 --> 00:13:07.411
and measure progress
and make sense of our lives.

00:13:07.745 --> 00:13:10.498
So maybe architecture can slow down

00:13:10.498 --> 00:13:13.501
our perception of time.

00:13:13.542 --> 00:13:17.922
Maybe it can make us pause
and maybe it can make us notice

00:13:17.922 --> 00:13:21.175
the moments that usually go unnoticed.

00:13:28.265 --> 00:13:31.185
You know, this whole craze
about the circadian rhythm,

00:13:31.185 --> 00:13:36.148
it's really all about resetting
our systems back to the natural state

00:13:36.524 --> 00:13:39.443
and realigning them
to the movement of the sun.

00:13:39.443 --> 00:13:43.823
I think as architects, we often design
for the hours and the days in our lives,

00:13:43.823 --> 00:13:46.826
but we often forget
about the seconds and minutes.

00:13:46.992 --> 00:13:49.537
So how do we make these more granular

00:13:49.537 --> 00:13:52.540
time intervals
more memorable in our lives?

00:13:52.665 --> 00:13:55.543
I think those are some of the questions
that we're going to try to answer

00:13:55.543 --> 00:13:57.253
as we progress the design.

00:13:57.253 --> 00:14:01.340
We're still in the schematic design phase,
so things are still really conceptual

00:14:01.340 --> 00:14:04.051
and there's still a lot of things
to figure out.

00:14:04.051 --> 00:14:06.720
And in fact, since making this video,

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:09.723
the design
has actually changed quite a bit.

00:14:09.849 --> 00:14:13.644
And because our business
is not just architecture,

00:14:13.644 --> 00:14:16.438
it does take a little bit longer
for us to do things.

00:14:16.438 --> 00:14:19.942
So please
be patient with the project updates.

00:14:20.651 --> 00:14:23.529
But that's also one of the best parts
for me that we can explore

00:14:23.529 --> 00:14:26.532
some of these concepts
a little bit more in depth.

00:14:26.949 --> 00:14:30.327
If you're interested in some of the assets
that we're using for the visuals

00:14:30.494 --> 00:14:34.456
like some of these watercolor people
will leave a link in the description.

00:14:34.874 --> 00:14:36.041
Let me know in the comments.

00:14:36.041 --> 00:14:37.626
What do you think about the project

00:14:37.626 --> 00:14:40.796
and what other concepts do
you think we should explore?

00:14:41.046 --> 00:14:43.465
And with that,
I'll see you guys in the next video.

